Z||000000||RFMike||09-03-99||05:43 PM||||What does ceramic coating do for headers, and is it worth the extra cash?
Z||000001||Phantom Rage||09-03-99||10:00 PM||||I believe the theory is that it keeps the heat inside your header(s), thus increasing a better scavenging effect.  They also will keep your header(s) from rusting, at least for a couple of years.  Rust seems to occur relatively quickly due to heating (expanding) and cooling (contracting) of the metal, combined with moisture in the air.  The coating also lowers under hood temp.  The header(s) look pretty nice after they are coated.  The lower under hood temp should help your air intake charge a little.  I can't remember the exact numbers, but here goes: For every 10 degrees less (intake charge) you will pickup 1-% hp.
Z||000002||Phantom Rage||09-03-99||10:04 PM||||Opps, "Is it worth it", I would say yes.  I paid $105 to get it done.  Heat wraps are by far cheaper, but stay away from them, for some reason they tend to make metal warp.<br>Hope this helps.
Z||000003||RFMike||09-04-99||08:56 AM||||Thanks, PR.  The last car I had headers on was a 71 camaro.  They were Hookers, w/o coating.  I didn't have any problem with rusting at all.  That was 24 years ago.  Has the quality of headers changed in that time frame?
Z||000004||PaUL||09-04-99||04:46 PM||||Yes the wrap is crap, dont get it. It becomes really dusty and deteriotes with age. It also makes your arms itch after working on the engine.
